{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Root cause found, and it is upstream — the ticket's premise was wrong\n\nPlan review blocked this on a misidentified distribution, which was correct and led directly to the actual cause. The remedy in the original plan would not have worked.\n\n### What was verified\n\n**1. The pin is `pypandoc-binary==1.17`, not `pypandoc`.** These are distinct distributions. `pyproject.toml:196` — `wiki = [\"pypandoc-binary==1.17\"]` — and the file's own comment states the distinction plainly: `pypandoc-binary` **bundles** the pandoc executable in its wheel, unlike bare `pypandoc`, which needs a system pandoc.\n\n**2. An arm64 macOS wheel exists at the pinned version.** PyPI publishes `pypandoc_binary-1.17-py3-none-macosx_11_0_arm64.whl` for 1.17. The pin is not the problem.\n\n**3. The correct wheel was installed.** The venv's own metadata says so:\n\n```\n.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/pypandoc_binary-1.17.dist-info/WHEEL\n  Tag: py3-none-macosx_11_0_arm64\nINSTALLER: uv\n```\n\nThe interpreter is arm64 (`platform.machine()` reports `arm64`, `file .venv/bin/python3` reports `Mach-O 64-bit executable arm64`), the host is arm64, and `uv` resolved and installed the arm64 wheel. Nothing in our toolchain chose wrongly.\n\n**4. The arm64 wheel contains an x86_64 binary.**\n\n```\nfile .venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/pypandoc/files/pandoc\n  Mach-O 64-bit executable x86_64\n```\n\nVerified against a **fresh download** rather than the local install, to rule out a corrupted or cross-contaminated venv:\n\n```\nsha256  fcfd28f347ed998dda28823fc6bc24f9310e7fdf3ddceaf925bf0563a100ab5b\nfile    x/pypandoc/files/pandoc: Mach-O 64-bit executable x86_64\nsize    119565632\nmtime   Feb  2 2020\n```\n\nThat hash matches `uv.lock:2399` exactly, so this is the precise artifact the lock pins — not a substitute.\n\n### Conclusion\n\n**`pypandoc-binary==1.17`'s `macosx_11_0_arm64` wheel ships an x86_64 pandoc executable.** This is an upstream packaging defect. Every layer under our control is correct: the pin, the lock, the resolver, the interpreter, and the installed wheel tag.\n\nThe embedded binary's mtime of **2020-02-02**, against a 1.17 upload time of 2026-03-14, is consistent with the arm64 wheel having been built by copying the x86_64 payload rather than an arm64 build.\n\n### What this invalidates\n\n- The original approach opened by asking whether the pinned distribution \"publishes an arm64 wheel or an arm64-capable binary.\" It publishes an arm64 **wheel** that is not arm64-**capable**. Those came apart, and the plan assumed they could not.\n- \"Repackaging the dependency for arm64\" is not available to us. The defect is in the artifact, so no resolver, lock, or install change reaches it.\n- The `985f` census framing of this as a `shutil.which` problem was wrong twice over: there is no such check, and the resolution path is not at fault either.\n\n### What remains open\n\nWhether a different pinned version ships a correct arm64 wheel is **not yet established** and is the first thing the remedy must settle. It carries real cost: ADR 0095 pins the version exactly because pandoc's `jira` writer changes its escaping between releases and the renderer's safety tests assert byte-level output. Moving the pin to fix the architecture may move the rendered bytes, which is precisely what the pin exists to prevent.\n\nNo files were changed and nothing was installed. The wheel was downloaded to a per-invocation scratch directory and deleted."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789695325945929786,"uuid":"8cd8be51-62b0-491b-85c1-b3e0a2567c7e"}